    'dwz.hero.breadcrumb':    'Finance Books',
    'dwz.hero.tag':           'Finance Books · ETFs · Strategy',
    'dwz.hero.title':         '<em>Die With Zero</em> — the book that<br>changed everything in my strategy',
    'dwz.meta.readTime':      ' 8 min read',
    'dwz.meta.tags':          'Finance Books · ETFs',
    'dwz.lead':               '"I had saved $200,000. I had a plan toward the million. And yet, I still felt guilty spending $50 at a restaurant. This book freed me from that anxiety."',
    'dwz.p1':                 'I\'ll be honest with you — for a long time I believed the more I accumulated, the safer I was. Every dollar spent felt like a dollar less for my retirement. I stressed. I calculated. I sometimes deprived myself unnecessarily.',
    'dwz.p2':                 'Then I read <strong>Die With Zero by Bill Perkins</strong>. And something changed.',
    'dwz.h2.book':            'What the book says — in brief',
    'dwz.p3':                 'Die With Zero starts from a simple but unsettling premise: <strong>dying with a lot of money means you lived poorly.</strong>',
    'dwz.p4':                 'Every unspent dollar represents wasted life energy. Time spent working for nothing. Experiences not lived. Trips not taken. Moments with loved ones you never had.',
    'dwz.p5':                 'This isn\'t a book that says "spend everything and don\'t save." It\'s much more nuanced than that.',
    'dwz.lecon1.title':       'The right money at the right time',
    'dwz.lecon1.desc':        '$100,000 at 35 is worth much more than $100,000 at 75. Your body, your energy, your ability to enjoy — all of it decreases over time. Investing in experiences now is maximizing their value.',
    'dwz.lecon2.title':       'Memory Dividends',
    'dwz.lecon2.desc':        'A trip at 38 with your child gives you memories for 40 years. That same trip at 72 gives you memories for maybe 10 years. Experiences yield "memory dividends" — and the sooner you live them, the longer you benefit.',
    'dwz.lecon3.title':       'Calculate your wealth peak',
    'dwz.lecon3.desc':        'There is an optimal moment to start drawing down — not too early, not too late. The goal isn\'t to have as much as possible at 90, but to have enough at the right moment in your life.',
    'dwz.h2.applied':         'How I applied this to my life',
    'dwz.p6':                 'When I read this book, I had already saved nearly <strong>$200,000</strong> since the pandemic. My goal: reach $1 million for retirement.',
    'dwz.p7':                 'Mathematically, I realized something important: with this capital invested in ETFs at historical returns, <strong>my million is already on autopilot</strong>. Compound interest does the heavy lifting — with or without me.',
    'dwz.pullquote':          '"I realized that the money I invest now is to accelerate the process — not to survive. I can breathe. I can take vacations. I can live now."',
    'dwz.p8':                 'Before this book, I felt guilty spending. After, I understood that <strong>living and investing are not opposites</strong>. I keep investing $7,000/year in my TFSA. The rest? I live with it. Without guilt.',
    'dwz.p9':                 'Miami in a few years. Trips with my son. Experiences I would have postponed indefinitely out of fear of "spending unnecessarily."',
    'dwz.h2.etf':             'But what is an ETF — and why I use them',
    'dwz.p10':                'This book also pushed me to better understand <em>how</em> my money was working. And that\'s where ETFs come in.',
    'dwz.h3.def':             'The simple definition',
    'dwz.p11':                'An <strong>ETF (Exchange Traded Fund)</strong> is a basket of several hundred stocks that you buy in a single transaction. Instead of choosing ONE company and hoping it performs, you invest in hundreds at once.',
    'dwz.p12':                '<strong>Concrete example:</strong> QQC.F — the ETF I use — contains Apple, Nvidia, Microsoft, Amazon, Google, Tesla and 94 other companies. By buying ONE share of QQC.F, you own a fraction of all these companies.',
    'dwz.h3.compare':         'ETF vs Individual stock',
    'dwz.th.critere':         'Criterion',
    'dwz.th.etf':             'ETF (QQC.F)',
    'dwz.th.action':          'Individual stock',
    'dwz.td.divers':          'Diversification',
    'dwz.td.etf.divers':      '100+ companies ✅',
    'dwz.td.act.divers':      '1 single company ❌',
    'dwz.td.fees':            'Annual fees',
    'dwz.td.act.fees':        'Commissions per transaction',
    'dwz.td.risk':            'Risk',
    'dwz.td.etf.risk':        'Low to moderate ✅',
    'dwz.td.act.risk':        'High ❌',
    'dwz.td.return':          'Historical return',
    'dwz.td.etf.return':      '~14%/yr (NASDAQ 20 yrs)',
    'dwz.td.act.return':      'Variable — unpredictable',
    'dwz.td.analysis':        'Analysis required',
    'dwz.td.etf.analysis':    'None ✅',
    'dwz.td.act.analysis':    'A lot ❌',
    'dwz.h2.start':           'If you start with $50 every 2 weeks',
    'dwz.p13':                'You think $50 every 2 weeks isn\'t enough to change your life? Let the numbers speak.',
    'dwz.p14':                '<strong>$50 × 26 times/year = $1,300/year invested.</strong> Total over 20 years: <strong>$26,000 out of your pocket.</strong>',
    'dwz.proj.low.label':     'Conservative scenario',
    'dwz.proj.mid.label':     'Realistic scenario',
    'dwz.proj.high.label':    'Optimistic scenario',
    'dwz.proj.invested':      'Invested: $26,000',
    'dwz.highlight':          'You invest <strong>$26,000</strong> out of your pocket over 20 years — and you come out with between <strong>$59,000 and $118,000</strong> depending on returns. That\'s the magic of compound interest: your money generates interest, that interest generates interest, and it snowballs exponentially over time.',
    'dwz.h3.means':           'What that means concretely',
    'dwz.p15':                'At 14%/yr — the historical NASDAQ return over 20 years — your biweekly $50 becomes <strong>4.5x your investment</strong>. You put in $26,000. You get back $118,000.',
    'dwz.p16':                'And if you put that in your <strong>TFSA</strong>? Zero tax on those $92,000 in gains. 100% in your pocket.',
    'dwz.h2.conclusion':      'My conclusion — Die With Zero applied',
    'dwz.p17':                'This book didn\'t tell me to stop investing. It told me to invest <em>intelligently</em> — and to live <em>now</em>.',
    'dwz.p18':                'Today my strategy is simple:',
    'dwz.p19':                '&nbsp;<strong>$7,000/year in my TFSA</strong> → automatic, stress-free<br><strong>My capital works on its own</strong> → compound interest does the rest<br><strong>The rest of my income?</strong> → live, travel, enjoy',
    'dwz.p20':                'I don\'t need to stress. My million is on its way. And in the meantime, I\'m living my life.',
    'dwz.p21':                'That\'s real financial freedom.',
    'dwz.disclaimer':         '⚠️ Note: I am not a financial planner. This article shares my personal experience — not official financial advice. Consult a professional for your specific situation.',
    'dwz.sidebar.title':      'Open your TFSA',
    'dwz.sidebar.body':       'Start investing with Wealthsimple — free, simple, no hidden fees.',
